/**
  @page examples-eikonal-fmm Computing arrival times with fast marching
  @brief Propagate from one exact seed, compare with an analytic distance and check refinement.

  @tableofcontents
  @m_footernavigation

  @section examples-eikonal-fmm-equation Arrival time and speed

  A front propagates through the unit square with positive speed @f$F(x)@f$.
  The arrival time @f$T@f$ satisfies
  @f[
    F(x)\lVert\nabla T(x)\rVert=1,
    \qquad T(c)=0,\qquad c=(0.5,0.5).
  @f]
  For constant speed @f$F=s@f$, the exact solution is
  @f[
    T(x)=\frac{\lVert x-c\rVert}{s}.
  @f]
  Unit speed produces distance; doubling the speed halves every arrival time.
  This provides a direct check of the speed convention used by
  @ref Rodin::Eikonal::FMM.

  @section examples-eikonal-fmm-geometry Choose an exact seed

  The program uses triangular grids with 16, 32 and 64 intervals per axis.
  Each count is even, so the center is a mesh vertex at every refinement.
  Seeding a neighborhood around the center would prescribe zero time on a
  larger source set and change the reference problem.

  @snippet Models/Eikonal/FMM.cpp fmm-mesh

  Vertex-to-cell incidence supplies the local simplicial stencils.
  Vertex-to-vertex incidence supplies neighboring trial vertices. Both are
  prepared before seeding and propagation.

  @section examples-eikonal-fmm-propagation Propagate the front

  A scalar continuous @f$P_1@f$ field stores nodal arrival times. The source
  contains exactly one vertex. `seed()` sets its value to zero and initializes
  neighboring trial values; `solve()` repeatedly accepts the smallest trial
  arrival time and updates its neighbors.

  @snippet Models/Eikonal/FMM.cpp fmm-solve

  The callable returns propagation speed in distance per unit time. Supply
  finite, strictly positive values. Its reciprocal is the local slowness.
  This example uses constant speeds 1 and 2, with identical grids and seeds.

  @section examples-eikonal-fmm-result Compare with the analytic field

  @image html FMMArrival.png "Computed unit-speed arrival time on the 64-interval grid. Contours show equal arrival times. The dense triangle edges are omitted so that they do not obscure the field."

  For the nodal set @f$\mathcal V_h@f$, the program records
  @f[
    e_\infty=\max_{a\in\mathcal V_h}|T_h(a)-T(a)|,
    \qquad
    e_{\mathrm{mean}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal V_h|}
        \sum_{a\in\mathcal V_h}|T_h(a)-T(a)|.
  @f]
  These are nodal error measures. The arithmetic mean is independent of cell
  areas and differs from an integrated error norm.

  @snippet Models/Eikonal/FMM.cpp fmm-error

  The checked local results for unit speed are:

  | Intervals per axis | Vertices | Maximum nodal error | Mean nodal error |
  | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
  | 16 | 289 | @f$0.0474507@f$ | @f$0.0158231@f$ |
  | 32 | 1089 | @f$0.0299216@f$ | @f$0.0102179@f$ |
  | 64 | 4225 | @f$0.0182986@f$ | @f$0.00634955@f$ |

  Both errors decrease under refinement. With speed 2, every tabulated error
  is halved to printed precision. The point-source solution has a singular
  gradient at the center; this sequence supplies measured convergence for
  this setup, without assuming the interpolation order of a smooth field.

  The executable requires finite nonnegative arrival times, a unique source,
  strictly decreasing maximum errors and the loose regression envelope
  @f$e_\infty\leq 2h/s@f$. That envelope is a check for this example's grids.
  Assess other geometries and variable-speed fields against their own reference
  solutions or refinement studies.

  @section examples-eikonal-fmm-run Build and inspect

  Build the `Eikonal_FMM` target and run it in a scratch directory. It needs no
  input mesh. `FMMErrors.csv` contains all six refinement/speed results.
  `FMMArrival.xdmf` and its HDF5 companions contain the finest unit-speed
  computed field `arrival` and interpolated analytic nodal field `reference`.
  Open the XDMF in ParaView and select **Contour** on `arrival` to inspect
  the advancing fronts.

  @section examples-eikonal-fmm-full Complete program

  @snippet Models/Eikonal/FMM.cpp fmm-source

  @section examples-eikonal-fmm-next Distance fields in applications

  A zero-time vertex set defines an unsigned arrival-time problem. Signed
  level-set reconstruction additionally needs an inside/outside convention.
  The @ref guides-shape-optimization "shape optimization guide" describes
  transport and redistancing; @ref examples-swift-background shows how an
  analytic signed distance can drive mesh-interface fitting.

  @example Models/Eikonal/FMM.cpp
*/
